# {{SERVICE_NAME}}

Generated by `zengo init {{SERVICE_NAME}}`.

## Quick start

```bash
# from platform root (once)
mage zengo
export PATH="$PWD/.bin:$PATH"

cd {{SERVICE_NAME}}
mage up          # postgres + kafka + jaeger
mage dev         # watch + zengo gen + go run
mage build
./bin/{{SERVICE_NAME}} --version
```

New services scaffold with `zengo.textproto` by default. Use `zengo init --manifest-format yaml ...` if you prefer YAML.
The primary API layout is `api/hub/...`; legacy `api/vN/...` is not scaffolded by default.
If you enable `transports.rest`, keep `transports.grpc` enabled too: REST is served through `grpc-gateway` against the local gRPC listener.
Transport TLS can be configured through `transports.grpc.tls`, `transports.rest.tls`, and `transports.rest.grpc_client_tls`. Typed infra configs share `api/config/meta/tls.proto` and accept file paths or inline PEM.
Public REST surface includes versioned API routes and hub routes mounted separately under `/hub/...`.
REST services also get split OpenAPI specs in `gen/openapi/`.
Service-level generator overrides can be placed in `.zengo/templates/`.
For Postgres services, `zengo gen` manages a baseline `migrations/001_init.sql` from the hub proto persistence model declared through `(zengo.options.repository).model` and `(zengo.options.column)` annotations. Keep the generated header if you want rewrites; remove it to take ownership manually.

If the service includes a REST gateway, it also exposes build metadata on `GET /buildz`.

## Add components later

```bash
zengo add kafka observability grpc
zengo gen
```

## Checks

```bash
mage check       # manifest + buf lint/breaking + gen freshness + vet + tests
```

